return_type":"array|false"},"documentation":{"description":"Get network interfaces Returns an enumeration of network interfaces (adapters) on the local machine. /","params":[],"return_type":"array|false","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_accept_distributed_trace_headers","fqn":"newrelic_accept_distributed_trace_headers","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":25963,"end":26004}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"headers","declared_type":"array","phpdoc_type":"array","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"transport_type","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Accepts an array of distributed trace headers. Distributed tracing allows you to see the path that a request takes as it travels through a distributed system. When distributed tracing is enabled, use newrelic_accept_distributed_trace_headers to accept a payload of headers. These include both W3C Trace Context and New Relic distributed trace headers. It is possible to only accept only W3C Trace Context headers and disable the New Relic Distributed Tracing header via the newrelic.distributed_tracing_exclude_newrelic_header INI setting. Returns True if the headers were accepted successfully, otherwise returns False. /","params":[{"name":"headers","declared_type":"array","description":"An array containing distributed tracing headers."},{"name":"transport_type","declared_type":"string","description":"[optional] A string overriding the default transport type."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_accept_distributed_trace_payload","fqn":"newrelic_accept_distributed_trace_payload","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":26701,"end":26742}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"payload","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"void","phpdoc_return_type":"void"},"documentation":{"description":"Accepts a distributed trace payload. Distributed tracing allows you to see the path that a request takes as it travels through a distributed system. /","params":[{"name":"payload","declared_type":"string","description":"A JSON formatted string created by using newrelic_create_distributed_trace_payload."}],"return_type":"void","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_accept_distributed_trace_payload_httpsafe","fqn":"newrelic_accept_distributed_trace_payload_httpsafe","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":27630,"end":27680}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"httpsafe_payload","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"transport_type","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Accepts a distributed trace payload that includes an HTTPSafe (Base64 encoded) JSON string. Distributed tracing allows you to see the path that a request takes as it travels through a distributed system. Returns true to indicate success, or false if an error occurs. /","params":[{"name":"httpsafe_payload","declared_type":"string","description":"An HTTPSafe (Base64 encoded) JSON string representation of the payload."},{"name":"transport_type","declared_type":"string","description":"[optional] A string overriding the default transport type."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_add_custom_parameter","fqn":"newrelic_add_custom_parameter","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":1419,"end":1448}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"key","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"value","declared_type":"bool|float|int|string","phpdoc_type":"bool|float|int|string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Attaches a custom attribute (key/value pair) to the current transaction and the current span (if enabled). Add a custom attribute (a key and a value data pair) to the current web transaction. (The call name is newrelic_add_custom_parameter because \"custom attributes\" were previously called \"custom parameters.\") For example, you can add a customer's full name from your customer database. This attribute appears in any transaction trace that results from this transaction. You can also query the Transaction event for your custom attributes. If the value given is a float with a value of NaN, Infinity, denorm or negative zero, the behavior of this function is undefined. For other floating point values, the agent may discard 1 or more bits of precision (ULPs) from the given value. Returns true if the parameter was added successfully. Important: If you want to use your custom attributes, avoid using any of the reserved terms used by NRQL. /","params":[{"name":"key","declared_type":"string","description":"The name of the custom attribute. Only the first 255 characters are retained."},{"name":"value","declared_type":"bool|float|int|string","description":"The value to associate with this custom attribute."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_add_custom_span_parameter","fqn":"newrelic_add_custom_span_parameter","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":28879,"end":28913}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"key","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"value","declared_type":"bool|float|int|string","phpdoc_type":"bool|float|int|string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Attaches a custom attribute (key/value pair) to the current span. Add a custom attribute (a key and a value data pair) to the current span. (The call name is newrelic_add_custom_span_parameter because \"custom attributes\" were previously called \"custom parameters.\") For example, you can add a customer's full name from your customer database. This attribute appears in any span. You can also query the Span for your custom attributes. IMPORTANT: On spans, attributes added with newrelic_add_custom_span_parameter will take precedence over attributes added with newrelic_add_custom_parameter. IMPORTANT: If you want to use your custom attributes, avoid using any of the reserved terms used by NRQL. /","params":[{"name":"key","declared_type":"string","description":"The name of the custom attribute. Only the first 255 characters are retained."},{"name":"value","declared_type":"bool|float|int|string","description":"The value to associate with this custom attribute."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_add_custom_tracer","fqn":"newrelic_add_custom_tracer","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":2400,"end":2426}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"functionName","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Specify functions or methods for the agent to instrument with custom instrumentation. Specify functions or methods for the agent to target for custom instrumentation. This is the API equivalent of the newrelic.transaction_tracer.custom setting. You cannot apply custom tracing to internal PHP functions. The name can be formatted either as function_name for procedural functions, or as \"ClassName::method\" for methods. Both static and instance methods will be instrumented if the method syntax is used, and the class name must be fully qualified: it must include the full namespace if the class was defined within a namespace. This function will return true if the tracer was added successfully. /","params":[{"name":"functionName","declared_type":"string","description":null}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_background_job","fqn":"newrelic_background_job","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":3289,"end":3312}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"flag","declared_type":"bool","phpdoc_type":"bool","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"void","phpdoc_return_type":"void"},"documentation":{"description":"Manually specify that a transaction is a background job or a web transaction. Tell the agent to treat this \"web\" transaction as a \"non-web\" transaction (the APM UI separates web and non-web transactions, for example in the Transactions page). Call as early as possible. This is most commonly used for cron jobs or other long-lived background tasks. However, this call is usually unnecessary since the agent usually detects whether a transaction is a web or non-web transaction automatically. You can also reverse the functionality by setting the optional flag to false, which marks a \"non-web\" transaction as a \"web\" transaction. /","params":[{"name":"flag","declared_type":"bool","description":"[optional]"}],"return_type":"void","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_capture_params","fqn":"newrelic_capture_params","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":3821,"end":3844}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"enable_flag","declared_type":"bool","phpdoc_type":"bool","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"void","phpdoc_return_type":"void"},"documentation":{"description":"Enable or disable the capture of URL parameters. Enable or disable the capture of URL parameters (everything after the ? in the URL). This overrides the newrelic.capture_params config file setting. IMPORTANT: If you pass sensitive information directly in the URL, keep this disabled. /","params":[{"name":"enable_flag","declared_type":"bool","description":"[optional]"}],"return_type":"void","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_create_distributed_trace_payload","fqn":"newrelic_create_distributed_trace_payload","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":29522,"end":29563}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"newrelic\\DistributedTracePayload","phpdoc_return_type":null},"documentation":{"description":"Creates a distributed trace payload. Distributed tracing allows you to see the path that a request takes as it travels through a distributed system. /","params":[],"return_type":null,"var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_custom_metric","fqn":"newrelic_custom_metric","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":4853,"end":4875}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"metric_name","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"value","declared_type":"float","phpdoc_type":"float","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Add a custom metric (in milliseconds) to time a component of your app not captured by default. Name your custom metrics with a Custom/ prefix (for example, Custom/MyMetric). This helps the UI organize your custom metrics in one place, and it makes them easily findable via the Metric Explorer. Records timing in milliseconds. For example: a value of 4 is stored as .004 seconds in New Relic's systems. If the value is NaN, Infinity, denorm or negative zero, the behavior of this function is undefined. New Relic may discard 1 or more bits of precision (ULPs) from the given value. This function will return true if the metric was added successfully. /","params":[{"name":"metric_name","declared_type":"string","description":null},{"name":"value","declared_type":"float","description":null}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_disable_autorum","fqn":"newrelic_disable_autorum","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":5679,"end":5703}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"?bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool|null"},"documentation":{"description":"Disable automatic injection of the browser monitoring snippet on particular pages. This call disables automatic injection of the browser monitoring agent for the current transaction. Call as early as possible. You can use this call to remove the JavaScript if the insertion is causing problems or if you are serving pages to third-party services that do not allow JavaScript (for example, Google's accelerated mobile pages). Returns true if called within a New Relic transaction. Otherwise returns null if outside a transaction (for example, if newrelic_end_transaction() has been called). /","params":[],"return_type":"bool|null","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_enable_params","fqn":"newrelic_enable_params","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":5782,"end":5804}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":null,"phpdoc_return_type":null},"documentation":null,"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_end_of_transaction","fqn":"newrelic_end_of_transaction","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":6665,"end":6692}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"void","phpdoc_return_type":"void"},"documentation":{"description":"Stop timing the current transaction, but continue instrumenting it. Stop timing the web transaction immediately. Useful when a page is done with app code and is about to stream data (file download, audio or video streaming, and so on), and you don't want streaming time to count as part of the transaction run time. The agent sends data to the daemon at the end of the transaction. This is especially relevant when the time taken to complete the operation is completely outside the bounds of your application. For example, a user on a very slow connection may take a very long time to download even small files, and you wouldn't want that download time to skew the real transaction time. /","params":[],"return_type":"void","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_end_transaction","fqn":"newrelic_end_transaction","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":7698,"end":7722}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"ignore","declared_type":"bool","phpdoc_type":"bool","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Stop instrumenting the current transaction immediately. Stop instrumenting the current transaction immediately, and send the data to the daemon. This call simulates what the agent normally does when PHP terminates the current transaction. The most common use for this call is to improve instrumentation of command line scripts that handle job queue processing. Call this method at the end of a particular job, and then call newrelic_start_transaction() when a new task is pulled off the queue. Normally, when you end a transaction you want the agent to record the associated data. However, you can also discard the data by setting $ignore to true. This function will return true if the transaction was successfully ended and data was sent to the New Relic daemon. /","params":[{"name":"ignore","declared_type":"bool","description":"[optional]"}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_get_browser_timing_footer","fqn":"newrelic_get_browser_timing_footer","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":8546,"end":8580}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"includeTags","declared_type":"bool","phpdoc_type":"bool","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"string","phpdoc_return_type":"string"},"documentation":{"description":"Returns a browser monitoring snippet to inject at the end of the HTML output. Returns a JavaScript string to inject at the very end of the HTML output. Use this call with newrelic_get_browser_timing_header() to manually add browser monitoring to a webpage. If possible, New Relic recommends instead enabling browser monitoring via the New Relic UI or copy/pasting the JavaScript snippet. For instructions on using these options, see Browser monitoring and the PHP agent. If includeTags omitted or set to true, the returned JavaScript string will be enclosed in a \"script\"-tag. /","params":[{"name":"includeTags","declared_type":"bool","description":"[optional]"}],"return_type":"string","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_get_browser_timing_header","fqn":"newrelic_get_browser_timing_header","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":9394,"end":9428}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"includeTags","declared_type":"bool","phpdoc_type":"bool","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"string","phpdoc_return_type":"string"},"documentation":{"description":"Returns a browser monitoring snippet to inject in the head of your HTML output. Returns a JavaScript string to inject in the \"head\"-tag of your HTML output. Use this call with newrelic_get_browser_timing_footer to manually add browser monitoring to a webpage. If possible, New Relic recommends instead enabling browser monitoring via the New Relic UI or copy/pasting the JavaScript snippet. For instructions on using these options, see browser monitoring and PH If includeTags are omitted or set to true, the returned JavaScript string will be enclosed in a \"script\"-tag. /","params":[{"name":"includeTags","declared_type":"bool","description":null}],"return_type":"string","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_get_linking_metadata","fqn":"newrelic_get_linking_metadata","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":29978,"end":30007}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"array","phpdoc_return_type":"array"},"documentation":{"description":"Returns a collection of metadata necessary for linking data to a trace or an entity. This call returns an opaque map of key-value pairs that can be used to correlate this application to other data in the New Relic backend. /","params":[],"return_type":"array","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_get_trace_metadata","fqn":"newrelic_get_trace_metadata","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":30469,"end":30496}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"array","phpdoc_return_type":"array"},"documentation":{"description":"Returns an associative array containing the identifiers of the current trace and the parent span. Returns an associative array containing the identifiers of the current trace and the parent span. This information is useful for integrating with third party distributed tracing tools, such as Zipkin. /","params":[],"return_type":"array","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_ignore_apdex","fqn":"newrelic_ignore_apdex","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":9864,"end":9885}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"void","phpdoc_return_type":"void"},"documentation":{"description":"Ignore the current transaction when calculating Apdex. Ignores the current transaction when calculating your Apdex score. This is useful when you have either very short or very long transactions (such as file downloads) that can skew your Apdex score. /","params":[],"return_type":"void","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_ignore_transaction","fqn":"newrelic_ignore_transaction","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":10319,"end":10346}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"void","phpdoc_return_type":"void"},"documentation":{"description":"Do not instrument the current transaction. Do not generate data for this transaction. This is useful when you have transactions that are particularly slow for known reasons and you do not want them frequently generating transaction traces or skewing your site averages. /","params":[],"return_type":"void","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_insert_distributed_trace_headers","fqn":"newrelic_insert_distributed_trace_headers","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":31606,"end":31647}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"headers","declared_type":"array","phpdoc_type":"array","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Inserts W3C Trace Context headers and New Relic Distributed Tracing headers into an outbound array of headers. Use newrelic_insert_distributed_trace_headers to manually add distributed tracing headers an array of outbound headers. When Distributed Tracing is enabled, newrelic_insert_distributed_trace_headers will always insert W3C trace context headers. It also, by default, inserts the New Relic Distributed Tracing header, but this can be disabled via the newrelic.distributed_tracing_exclude_newrelic_header INI setting. The $headers argument is passed by reference, and therefore must be a variable as opposed to a literal. /","params":[{"name":"headers","declared_type":"array","description":"An (optionally empty) array of outbound headers."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_is_sampled","fqn":"newrelic_is_sampled","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":31909,"end":31928}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Returns a value indicating whether or not the current transaction is marked as sampled. /","params":[],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_name_transaction","fqn":"newrelic_name_transaction","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":11757,"end":11782}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"name","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Set custom name for current transaction. Sets the name of the transaction to the specified name. This can be useful if you have implemented your own dispatching scheme and want to name transactions according to their purpose. Call this function as early as possible. IMPORTANT: Do not use brackets [suffix] at the end of your transaction name. New Relic automatically strips brackets from the name. Instead, use parentheses (suffix) or other symbols if needed. Unique values like URLs, Page Titles, Hex Values, Session IDs, and uniquely identifiable values should not be used in naming your transactions. Instead, add that data to the transaction as a custom parameter with the newrelic_add_custom_parameter() call. IMPORTANT: Do not create more than 1000 unique transaction names (for example, avoid naming by URL if possible). This will make your charts less useful, and you may run into limits New Relic sets on the number of unique transaction names per account. It also can slow down the performance of your application. Returns true if the transaction name was successfully changed. If false is returned, check the agent log for more information. /","params":[{"name":"name","declared_type":"string","description":"Name of the transaction."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_notice_error","fqn":"newrelic_notice_error","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":14551,"end":14572}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"messageOrExceptionOrCode","declared_type":"string|Throwable|Exception|int","phpdoc_type":"string|Throwable|Exception|int","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"errstrOrException","declared_type":"string|Throwable|Exception|null","phpdoc_type":"string|Throwable|Exception|null","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"errfile","declared_type":"?string","phpdoc_type":"string|null","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"errline","declared_type":"?int","phpdoc_type":"int|null","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"errcontext","declared_type":"?string","phpdoc_type":"string|null","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":null,"phpdoc_return_type":"null"},"documentation":{"description":"Use these calls to collect errors that the PHP agent does not collect automatically and to set the callback for your own error and exception handler. CAUTION: If you include an exception ($e), there are differences depending on the PHP version you are using: - PHP version 5 or lower: You must pass a valid PHP Exception class. - PHP version 7 or higher: You must pass a valid PHP Throwable interface. The PHP agent handles PHP errors and exceptions automatically for supported frameworks. If you want to collect errors that are not handled automatically so that you can query for those errors in New Relic and view error traces, you can use newrelic_notice_error. If you want to use your own error and exception handlers, you can set newrelic_notice_error as the callback. This function can handle a variable number of parameters. You can pass-in 1 or 5 parameters, depending on your use case: - newrelic_notice_error(string $message) - newrelic_notice_error(Throwable|Exception $e) - newrelic_notice_error(string $errstr, Throwable|Exception $e) - newrelic_notice_error(int $errno, string $errstr, string $errfile, int $errline, string $errcontext) an integer error code integer, Provide an error message that will be meaningful to you when it displays in error traces or an exception object. occurred in. occurred. table that was active when the error occurred. /","params":[{"name":"messageOrExceptionOrCode","declared_type":"string|Throwable|Exception|int","description":"Either an error message, an exception object, or"},{"name":"errstrOrException","declared_type":"string|Throwable|Exception|null","description":"[optional] Required if first argument is an"},{"name":"errfile","declared_type":"string|null","description":"[optional] The name of the file that the error"},{"name":"errline","declared_type":"int|null","description":"[optional] The line number where the error"},{"name":"errcontext","declared_type":"string|null","description":"[optional] An array that points to the symbol"}],"return_type":"null","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_record_custom_event","fqn":"newrelic_record_custom_event","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":15982,"end":16010}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"name","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"attributes","declared_type":"array","phpdoc_type":"array","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"void","phpdoc_return_type":"void"},"documentation":{"description":"Record a custom event with the given name and attributes Records a custom event for use in New Relic. For more information, see Inserting custom events with the PHP agent. TIP: When creating custom events, follow these rules: - The agent records a maximum of 10,000 events per minute. Limit the number of unique event type names that you create, and do not generate these names dynamically. - Avoid using reserved words and characters for the event and attributes names. - Ensure you do not exceed the event size and rate restrictions. up to 255 characters, and supply the values as scalar values. Arrays and objects are not supported. /","params":[{"name":"name","declared_type":"string","description":"Name of the custom event."},{"name":"attributes","declared_type":"array","description":"Supply custom attributes as an associative array. Supply the attribute names as keys of"}],"return_type":"void","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_record_datastore_segment","fqn":"newrelic_record_datastore_segment","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":24786,"end":24819}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"func","declared_type":"callable","phpdoc_type":"callable","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"parameters","declared_type":"array","phpdoc_type":"array","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"mixed","phpdoc_return_type":"mixed"},"documentation":{"description":"Records a datastore segment. Datastore segments appear in the Breakdown table and Databases tab of the Transactions page in the New Relic UI. This function allows an unsupported datastore to be instrumented in the same way as the PHP agent automatically instruments its supported datastores. The supported keys in the $parameters array are as follows: - product: (string) Required. The name of the datastore product being used: for example, MySQL to indicate that the segment represents a query against a MySQL database. - collection: (string) Optional. The table or collection being used or queried against. - operation: (string) Optional. The operation being performed: for example, select for an SQL SELECT query, or set for a Memcached set operation. While operations may be specified with any case, New Relic suggests using lowercase to better line up with the operation names used by the PHP agent's automated datastore instrumentation. - host: (string) Optional. The datastore host name. - portPathOrId: (string) Optional. The port or socket used to connect to the datastore. - databaseName: (string) Optional. The database name or number in use. - query: (string) Optional. The query that was sent to the server. For security reasons, this value is only used if you set product to a supported datastore. This allows the agent to correctly obfuscate the query. The supported product values (which are matched in a case insensitive manner) are: MySQL, MSSQL, Oracle, Postgres, SQLite, Firebird, Sybase, and Informix. - inputQueryLabel: (string) Optional. The name of the ORM in use (for example: Doctrine). - inputQuery: (string) Optional. The input query that was provided to the ORM. For security reasons, and as with the query parameter, this value will be ignored if the product is not a supported datastore. an error at the E_WARNING level will be triggered /","params":[{"name":"func","declared_type":"callable","description":"The function that should be timed to create the datastore segment."},{"name":"parameters","declared_type":"array","description":null}],"return_type":"mixed","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_set_appname","fqn":"newrelic_set_appname","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":18958,"end":18978}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"name","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"license","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"xmit","declared_type":"bool","phpdoc_type":"bool","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Set the New Relic application name, which the New Relic UI uses to connect your data to the correct application. The recommended and preferred method for setting the New Relic application name is to use global or per-directory INI settings to associate your application to the desired name at the start of execution. If you cannot edit your agent config file (for example, many shared hosting environments do not allow you to edit config files), the newrelic_set_appname API call can be used to configure app name, license key (optional, for if you use multiple accounts), and a true/false flag (optional, to determine whether to keep or discard previously recorded data). To ensure maximal APM trace continuity, call this as early as possible. This API call will discard all current transaction data and start a new transaction after it has reconnected with the given app name. For other app-naming options, see Name your PHP application. When possible, INI-based solutions are recommended. New Relic highly recommends that you call this function as soon as possible and in as shallow of a call stack as possible. Applications are discrete entities in APM, and discontinuity of transaction traces is inherent to this method's usage. After connecting with the new app name, the new transactions start reporting to the new application without the context of the previous application or the data that has been reported to it. This method is intended to be called once, as each call to the API (even with the same app name) will cause the current transaction data to be discarded and lead to further discontinuity of transaction traces. This function can be called with 1 to 3 parameters: - newrelic_set_appname(string $name) - newrelic_set_appname(string $name, string $license[, bool $xmit]) format as newrelic.appname and can set multiple application names by separating each with a semi-colon ;. captured up to this call is lost. If true, the agent sends the data that was gathered right before executing this call. The data is associated with the old app name. This has a very slight performance impact as it takes a few milliseconds for the agent to dump its data. /","params":[{"name":"name","declared_type":"string","description":"Name(s) of app metrics should be reported under in New Relic user interface. Uses the same"},{"name":"license","declared_type":"string","description":"[optional] Required if you use multiple accounts"},{"name":"xmit","declared_type":"bool","description":"[optional] If false or omitted, the agent discards the current transaction and all data"}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_set_user_attributes","fqn":"newrelic_set_user_attributes","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":20356,"end":20384}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"user_value","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"account_value","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"product_value","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Create user-related custom attributes. newrelic_add_custom_parameter is more flexible. TIP: This call only allows you to assign values to pre-existing keys. For a more flexible method to create key/value pairs, use newrelic_add_custom_parameter. As of release 4.4, calling newrelic_set_user_attributes(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"); is equivalent to calling newrelic_add_custom_parameter(\"user\", \"a\"); newrelic_add_custom_parameter(\"account\", \"b\"); newrelic_add_custom_parameter(\"product\", \"c\"); All three parameters are required, but they may be empty strings. to the user key. assigned to the account key. assigned to the product key. /","params":[{"name":"user_value","declared_type":"string","description":"Specify a name or username to associate with this page view. This value is assigned"},{"name":"account_value","declared_type":"string","description":"Specify the name of a user account to associate with this page view. This value is"},{"name":"product_value","declared_type":"string","description":"Specify the name of a product to associate with this page view. This value is"}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_set_user_id","fqn":"newrelic_set_user_id","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":20887,"end":20907}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"user_id","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Associate a unique user ID with the current transaction. This function will return true if the user ID was added successfully, otherwise false. /","params":[{"name":"user_id","declared_type":"string","description":"Should be a unique value (such as a UUID) that will be assigned to the current transaction."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"newrelic_start_transaction","fqn":"newrelic_start_transaction","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"newrelic","location":{"file":"newrelic/newrelic.php","range":{"start":22381,"end":22407}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"appname","declared_type":"string","phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"license","declared_type":"?string","phpdoc_type":"string|null","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":"bool","phpdoc_return_type":"bool"},"documentation":{"description":"Start a new transaction manually. Usually used after manually ending a transaction with newrelic_end_transaction(), for example when separating tasks in a job queue manager. When instrumenting this new transaction, the agent performs the same operations as when the script first started. This function will return true if the transaction was successfully started. format as newrelic.appname and can set multiple application names by separating each with a semicolon ;. While this option is required, you can read the app name from newrelic.ini with ini_get(\"newrelic.appname\"). Provide a different license key if you want the transaction to report to a different New Relic account. If set, this license will supersede all per-directory and global default licenses configured in INI files. /","params":[{"name":"appname","declared_type":"string","description":"The application name to associate with data from this transaction. Uses the same"},{"name":"license","declared_type":"string|null","description":"[optional] Defaults to the license key set in the New Relic agent's newrelic.ini file."}],"return_type":"bool","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"next","fqn":"next","kind":"Function","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"standard","location":{"file":"standard/standard_8.php","range":{"start":24224,"end":24228}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"array","declared_type":"object|array","phpdoc_type":null,"optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":true}],"declared_return_type":"mixed","phpdoc_return_type":"mixed|false"},"documentation":{"description":"Advance the internal array pointer of an array The array being affected. </p> internal array pointer, or false if there are no more elements. /","params":[{"name":"","declared_type":null,"description":null}],"return_type":"mixed|false","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"ngettext","fqn":"ngettext","kind":"Function","orig