r is the first parameter to the function.</p></blockquote> /","params":[{"name":"function_name","declared_type":"string","description":"<p>The name of the function used in SQL statements.</p>"},{"name":"callback","declared_type":"callable","description":"<p>"},{"name":"num_args","declared_type":"int","description":"[optional]   <blockquote><p><b>Note</b>: Two alternative syntaxes are"}],"return_type":null,"var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"fetchColumnTypes","fqn":"SQLiteDatabase::fetchColumnTypes","kind":"Method","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"SQLite","location":{"file":"SQLite/SQLite.php","range":{"start":12523,"end":12539}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"table_name","declared_type":null,"phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"result_type","declared_type":null,"phpdoc_type":"int","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false}],"declared_return_type":null,"phpdoc_return_type":"array"},"documentation":{"description":"(PHP 5 &lt; 5.4.0) Return an array of column types from a particular table The optional <i>result_type</i> parameter accepts a constant and determines how the returned array will be indexed. Using <b>SQLITE_ASSOC</b> will return only associative indices (named fields) while <b>SQLITE_NUM</b> will return only numerical indices (ordinal field numbers). <b>SQLITE_ASSOC</b> is the default for this function. </p> Returns an array of column data types; <b>FALSE</b> on error. </p> <p>The column names returned by <b>SQLITE_ASSOC</b> and <b>SQLITE_BOTH</b> will be case-folded according to the value of the {@link https://php.net/manual/en/sqlite.configuration.php#ini.sqlite.assoc-case sqlite.assoc_case} configuration option.</p> /","params":[{"name":"table_name","declared_type":"string","description":"<p>The table name to query.</p>"},{"name":"result_type","declared_type":"int","description":"[optional] <p>"}],"return_type":"array","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"lastError","fqn":"SQLiteDatabase::lastError","kind":"Method","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"SQLite","location":{"file":"SQLite/SQLite.php","range":{"start":11409,"end":11418}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":null,"phpdoc_return_type":"int"},"documentation":{"description":"(PHP 5 &lt; 5.4.0, PECL sqlite &gt;= 1.0.0) Returns the error code of the last error for a database /","params":[],"return_type":"int","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"lastInsertRowid","fqn":"SQLiteDatabase::lastInsertRowid","kind":"Method","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"SQLite","location":{"file":"SQLite/SQLite.php","range":{"start":8198,"end":8213}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[],"declared_return_type":null,"phpdoc_return_type":"int"},"documentation":{"description":"(PHP 5 &lt; 5.4.0, PECL sqlite &gt;= 1.0.0) Returns the rowid of the most recently inserted row /","params":[],"return_type":"int","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"query","fqn":"SQLiteDatabase::query","kind":"Method","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"SQLite","location":{"file":"SQLite/SQLite.php","range":{"start":3093,"end":3098}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"query","declared_type":null,"phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"result_type","declared_type":null,"phpdoc_type":"int","optional":true,"variadic":false,"by_reference":false},{"name":"error_message","declared_type":null,"phpdoc_type":null,"optional":false,"variadic":false,"by_reference":true}],"declared_return_type":null,"phpdoc_return_type":"resource|false"},"documentation":{"description":"(PHP 5 &lt; 5.4.0, PECL sqlite &gt;= 1.0.0) The query to be executed. </p> <p> Data inside the query should be {@link https://php.net/manual/en/function.sqlite-escape-string.php properly escaped}. </p> <p>The optional <i>result_type</i> parameter accepts a constant and determines how the returned array will be indexed. Using <b>SQLITE_ASSOC</b> will return only associative indices (named fields) while <b>SQLITE_NUM</b> will return only numerical indices (ordinal field numbers). <b>SQLITE_BOTH</b> will return both associative and numerical indices. <b>SQLITE_BOTH</b> is the default for this function.</p> This function will return a result handle or <b>FALSE</b> on failure. For queries that return rows, the result handle can then be used with functions such as {@see sqlite_fetch_array()} and {@see sqlite_seek()}. </p> <p> Regardless of the query type, this function will return <b>FALSE</b> if the query failed. </p> <p> {@see sqlite_query()} returns a buffered, seekable result handle.  This is useful for reasonably small queries where you need to be able to randomly access the rows.  Buffered result handles will allocate memory to hold the entire result and will not return until it has been fetched.  If you only need sequential access to the data, it is recommended that you use the much higher performance {@see sqlite_unbuffered_query()} instead. </p> /","params":[{"name":"query","declared_type":"string","description":"<p>"},{"name":"result_type","declared_type":"int","description":"[optional]"},{"name":"","declared_type":null,"description":null}],"return_type":"resource|false","var_type":null,"throws":[],"deprecated":null},"availability":{"since":null,"until":null},"is_static":false},{"name":"queryExec","fqn":"SQLiteDatabase::queryExec","kind":"Method","origin":"PhpRuntime","extension":"SQLite","location":{"file":"SQLite/SQLite.php","range":{"start":4275,"end":4284}},"declared_type":null,"signature":{"parameters":[{"name":"query","declared_type":null,"phpdoc_type":"string","optional":false,"vari